WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:01.889
The global movement against free

00:00:01.890 --> 00:00:03.839
trade and capitalism that first came

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:05.909
together at the 1999 protest

00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:07.979
against the World Trade Organization

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:09.719
coalesced around the idea that

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:11.669
Western elites had led third world

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:13.589
nations to get ensnared in a debt

00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:15.539
trap as a way to coerce them

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:18.209
into adopting neoliberal policies.

00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:20.129
When poorer nations couldn't afford

00:00:20.130 --> 00:00:21.779
to repay what they owed the World

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:23.399
Bank and the International Monetary

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:25.169
Fund, these Washington based

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:27.329
organizations agreed to restructure

00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:29.309
the loans of debtor countries if

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:30.599
they would agree to move in the

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:32.099
direction of privatization,

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:34.019
deregulation and free trade.

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:36.209
A policy agenda termed "shock

00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:38.159
therapy." Leftist leaders

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:39.869
like Hugo Chavez claimed that this

00:00:39.870 --> 00:00:41.309
was part of a pattern of elite

00:00:41.310 --> 00:00:43.139
subjugation of Latin America that

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:44.579
stretched all the way back to

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:46.049
Christopher Columbus.

00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:47.909
Chavez used this as evidence in

00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:49.889
support of Venezuela's turn

00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:51.690
toward Cuba-style socialism.

00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:15.440
Columbia

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:20.939
University's Joseph Stiglitz, a

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:22.709
former chief economist at the World

00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:24.569
Bank and a Nobel Prize-winning

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:26.519
critic of free market capitalism,

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:28.109
promoted the same narratives.

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:30.209
He blamed the Washington consensus

00:01:30.210 --> 00:01:32.129
for pushing policies he deemed too

00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:33.959
radical and wrongheaded to ever get

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:35.339
through in the U.S.

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:37.229
What is your criticism of the

00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:39.209
World Bank and the IMF

00:01:39.210 --> 00:01:41.069
and all that they were doing?

00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:43.139
In many ways, they were pursuing

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:44.969
policies, pushing

00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:45.989
developing countries to have

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:47.879
policies that were very

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:49.859
different from the policies that

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:51.899
we were working for or supported

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:53.819
at home. I mean, for instance,

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:55.919
privatization of Social Security.

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:57.389
So like, generally speaking, IMF

00:01:57.390 --> 00:01:59.249
programs are designed to prevent

00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:01.859
Western-style social democracies.

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:03.119
They're designed to prevent free

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:04.229
health care and education.

00:02:04.230 --> 00:02:05.999
They're designed to prevent even

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:08.129
modest subsidies on basic

00:02:08.130 --> 00:02:08.698
goods.

00:02:08.699 --> 00:02:10.978
Unlike Stiglitz, Alex Gladstein

00:02:10.979 --> 00:02:12.779
identifies as a classical liberal

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:14.309
and the Human Rights Foundation,

00:02:14.310 --> 00:02:16.049
where he works as chief strategy

00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:17.879
officer, condemns the crimes

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:19.589
committed by socialist dictators

00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:20.549
like Chavez.

00:02:20.550 --> 00:02:22.319
Yet in his recent book, "Hidden

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:24.269
Repression: How the IMF and World

00:02:24.270 --> 00:02:25.739
Bank so Exploitation is

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:27.419
Development," Gladstein argues that

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:29.519
the story told by the radical left

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:31.559
about these two organizations is

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:32.939
largely correct.

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:34.979
His work over the last few years

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:36.929
has focused heavily on monetary

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:39.029
colonialism, or how the US

00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:40.859
and European nations use their

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:42.869
control of global currency to

00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:44.519
override the sovereignty of the

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:46.409
poorer nations in Latin America

00:02:46.410 --> 00:02:47.339
and Africa.

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:49.199
The fix, he says, is for

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:51.119
the world to transition to Bitcoin,

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:52.679
a form of freedom money that no

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:54.119
country or corporation can

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:55.769
manipulate or control.

00:02:55.770 --> 00:02:57.629
Reason sat down with Gladstein at

00:02:57.630 --> 00:02:59.459
the Miami Bitcoin Conference in May

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:00.900
to talk about his new book.

00:03:06.250 --> 00:03:07.579
Why did you write Hidden Repression

00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:09.609
and what is monetary colonialism and

00:03:09.610 --> 00:03:10.610
why is it bad?

00:03:11.430 --> 00:03:12.809
I wrote Hidden Repression because I

00:03:12.810 --> 00:03:14.309
had a lot of questions about the IMF

00:03:14.310 --> 00:03:15.629
and World Bank.

00:03:15.630 --> 00:03:16.630
I

00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:19.139
knew that a lot of people in the

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:21.509
Global South didn't like the IMF.

00:03:21.510 --> 00:03:23.250
I knew that it had a bad reputation.

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:25.289
I didn't have the same view about

00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:26.519
the World Bank, I felt like most

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:27.779
people talked about the World Bank

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:30.509
as maybe a neutral institution,

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:31.889
maybe had some issues, but generally

00:03:31.890 --> 00:03:34.049
speaking, did good things.

00:03:34.050 --> 00:03:35.879
But I started to kind

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:37.229
of just notice that they were tied

00:03:37.230 --> 00:03:39.269
to certain practices

00:03:39.270 --> 00:03:40.469
and certain phenomena that I was

00:03:40.470 --> 00:03:42.689
writing about, most notably

00:03:42.690 --> 00:03:45.509
monetary colonialism in West Africa.

00:03:45.510 --> 00:03:47.059
I noticed that the IMF was kind of

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:48.419
an instrument that the French used

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:50.189
to kind of impose currency

00:03:50.190 --> 00:03:51.674
devaluation on this subject,

00:03:51.675 --> 00:03:52.675
post-colonial

00:03:53.970 --> 00:03:55.499
countries there.

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:56.489
And I started to look around for

00:03:56.490 --> 00:03:58.199
resources last summer, and I noticed

00:03:58.200 --> 00:03:59.838
that there was no kind of pop econ

00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:03.479
book about the IMF, that that

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:04.439
was approachable.

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:05.759
Like there's no "Big Short" for the

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:07.229
IMF.

00:04:07.230 --> 00:04:08.429
There is nothing I mean, seriously,

00:04:08.430 --> 00:04:09.269
there's really nothing.

00:04:09.270 --> 00:04:11.369
There's. There's the shock doctrine

00:04:11.370 --> 00:04:13.229
and there's like "Confessions

00:04:13.230 --> 00:04:15.059
of an Economic Hitman," which are

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:17.129
just not really

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:18.018
what I needed.

00:04:18.019 --> 00:04:19.319
You know, the second one is like,

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:20.879
highly exaggerated and who knows how

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:22.078
true it is.

00:04:22.079 --> 00:04:23.909
And they're just they were very kind

00:04:23.910 --> 00:04:26.849
of one-sided political texts.

00:04:26.850 --> 00:04:28.949
So I just started to say,

00:04:28.950 --> 00:04:30.119
all right, well, let me dig into

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:31.199
this. And I started to read

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:32.569
everything I could find online.

00:04:32.570 --> 00:04:34.769
I fall back like lots of books,

00:04:34.770 --> 00:04:36.949
tons of JSTOR articles,

00:04:36.950 --> 00:04:39.389
a lot of credit to CI HUB.

00:04:39.390 --> 00:04:40.799
Definitely support the Communists in

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:42.719
Kazakhstan who run CI HUB.

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:44.549
You can give them bitcoin to

00:04:44.550 --> 00:04:46.139
get all this free articles that

00:04:46.140 --> 00:04:47.039
otherwise are behind this like

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:48.160
insane firewall.

00:04:49.890 --> 00:04:51.539
But just tons of like JSTOR articles

00:04:51.540 --> 00:04:53.309
from the sixties and seventies and

00:04:53.310 --> 00:04:54.209
eighties.

00:04:54.210 --> 00:04:55.319
Lots of books written in the

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:56.999
eighties and nineties.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:04:58.289
And then there's just like it just

00:04:58.290 --> 00:04:59.290
sort of ended.

